QB.add(37,[{"n":1,"q":"In which of the following conditions is pneumomediastinum commonly found?","o":[{"t":"Pulsion diverticulum"},{"t":"Boerhaave syndrome"},{"t":"Mallory-Weiss syndrome"},{"t":"Pericarditis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Boerhaave syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is characterized by a full-thickness rupture of the esophagus, usually in the lower third."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The rupture allows the esophageal contents to spill into the mediastinal cavity, which can lead to pneumomediastinum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is often accompanied by severe chest pain due to chemical mediastinitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pulsion diverticulum: This condition, such as Zenker's diverticulum, occurs due to increased intraluminal pressure in the esophagus, leading to the formation of a diverticulum. It does not typically cause pneumomediastinum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Mallory-Weiss syndrome : This syndrome involves tears in the mucosa of the esophagus, often due to severe retching or vomiting, typically associated with binge drinking. It can lead to hematemesis (vomiting of blood) but does not typically cause pneumomediastinum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pericarditis: This is inflammation of the pericardium, the sac surrounding the heart. While it causes chest pain and other symptoms related to inflammation, it does not result in pneumomediastinum."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Which of the following symptoms suggests the use of steroids in IgA vasculitis?","o":[{"t":"Joint inflammation"},{"t":"Gastrointestinal hemorrhage"},{"t":"Abdominal discomfort"},{"t":"Dermatological eruption"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Gastrointestinal hemorrhage:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Steroids are administered in cases of gastrointestinal bleeding or suspected perforation to mitigate inflammation and reduce the risk of complications."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C & D are incorrect."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 42-year-old female with complaints of recurrent episodes of abdominal pain and cramps. Endoscopy is done and diagnosed as UC. She currently has 3-4 episodes of bloody stools per day. Appropriate initial treatment will be?","o":[{"t":"Budesonide"},{"t":"Sulfasalazine"},{"t":"Azathioprine"},{"t":"Bezlotoxumab"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B – Sulfasalazine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical picture of this patient is suggestive of mild ulcerative colitis, and for mild ulcerative colitis - Sulfasalazine is used"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A – Budesonide: It is used in cases of exacerbation of ulcerative colitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C – Azathioprine: Azathioprine (AZA) is a medication used in the management and treatment of active rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and the prevention of kidney transplant rejection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bezlotoxumab: Bezlotoxumab is indicated to reduce the recurrence of Clostridium difficile infection (CDI) in patients who are receiving antibacterial drug treatment for CDI and are at high risk for CDI recurrence."}]},{"n":4,"q":"Which of the following diseases is associated with ATP7A gene causing low levels of copper in the blood stream?","o":[{"t":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome"},{"t":"Wilson’s disease"},{"t":"Menke’s disease"},{"t":"Gilbert’s syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C: Menke's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Menke's disease, also known as Menkes syndrome or copper transport disease, is a rare X-linked recessive disorder characterized by impaired copper absorption and transport."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is caused by mutations in the ATP7A gene, which encodes a copper-transporting ATPase involved in the transport of copper into the cells."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Dubin-Johnson syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by chronic jaundice due to impaired excretion of conjugated bilirubin. It is not related to copper metabolism or the ATP7A gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Wilson's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wilson's disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the ATP7B gene, leading to impaired copper transport and accumulation of copper in the liver and brain. It is associated with high levels of copper in tissues and low levels in the blood, but it is not caused by mutations in the ATP7A gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Gilbert's syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is typically inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The condition is caused by mutations in the UGT1A1 gene, which leads to reduced activity of the enzyme uridine diphosphate-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1). This enzyme is crucial for converting bilirubin into a form that can be excreted from the body."}]},{"n":5,"q":"In which condition are macrophages observed to be positive for periodic acid Schiff staining?","o":[{"t":"Agammaglobulinemia"},{"t":"Whipple’s disease"},{"t":"Abetalipoproteinemia"},{"t":"Crohn’s disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Whipple's disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Whipple's disease is a rare systemic infectious disorder caused by the bacterium Tropheryma whipplei ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized by infiltration of small intestinal mucosa by ‘foamy macrophages’, which stain positive with periodic acid-schiff (PAS) reagent."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Agammaglobulinemia: This statement is incorrect. Agammaglobulinemia refers to a group of primary immunodeficiency disorders characterized by a lack of mature B cells and reduced or absent immunoglobulin production."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Abetalipoproteinemia: This statement is incorrect. Abetalipoproteinemia is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the absence of beta-lipoproteins, leading to impaired absorption and transport of dietary fats and fat-soluble vitamins."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Crohn's disease: This statement is incorrect. While Crohn's disease is associated with the presence of macrophages and other immune cells in the affected intestinal tissue, PAS-positive macrophages are not a specific feature of Crohn's disease."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 22-year-old man presented with diarrhea and intolerance to dairy products. On investigation, he was found to have a lactase deficiency. Which of the following agents is least likely to cause symptoms of lactose intolerance?","o":[{"t":"Condensed Milk"},{"t":"Skimmed Milk"},{"t":"Yogurt"},{"t":"Ice Cream"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Yogurt"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lactose intolerance is a condition characterized by the inability to digest lactose, a sugar found in dairy products caused due to deficiency of lactase responsible to breakdown lactose."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Yogurt is produced through the fermentation of milk by bacteria, typically Lactobacillus and Streptococcus species. During the fermentation process, these bacteria produce lactase, which helps break down lactose into simpler sugars. As a result, yogurt contains lower levels of lactose compared to other dairy products."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Condensed Milk: Condensed milk is milk from which water has been removed, resulting in a concentrated form. It contains a high concentration of lactose, and therefore, is likely to cause symptoms of lactose intolerance in individuals with lactase deficiency."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Skimmed Milk: Skimmed milk refers to milk from which the cream (high-fat portion) has been removed. While the fat content is reduced, lactose content remains relatively unchanged. Therefore, skimmed milk can still cause symptoms of lactose intolerance in individuals with lactase deficiency."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ice Cream: Ice cream is a dairy product that typically contains high amounts of lactose. Therefore, ice cream is likely to cause symptoms of lactose intolerance in individuals with lactase deficiency."}]},{"n":7,"q":"What is the most common immediate complication following a splenectomy?","o":[{"t":"Hemorrhage"},{"t":"Fistula"},{"t":"Bleeding from gastric mucosa"},{"t":"Pancreatitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hemorrhage:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is the correct option. Hemorrhage, or bleeding, is one of the most common immediate complications of splenectomy. The spleen is a highly vascular organ, and during surgery, there is a risk of bleeding from the splenic artery or its branches. Measures are taken to control bleeding during and after the procedure, but it remains a potential complication."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Fistula: While it is possible for a fistula to develop after splenectomy, it is not the most common immediate complication. Therefore, option B is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Bleeding from gastric mucosa: The spleen is not directly involved in regulating gastric mucosal integrity or bleeding control. Therefore, option C is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pancreatitis: While complications related to the pancreas can occur after splenectomy in some cases, pancreatitis itself is not the most common immediate complication. Therefore, option D is incorrect."}]},{"n":8,"q":"In what situations is surgery contraindicated for patients with Ulcerative Colitis?","o":[{"t":"Crohn’s disease"},{"t":"Dysplasia"},{"t":"Toxic megacolon"},{"t":"Colonic bleeding"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Crohn’s disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crohn’s disease: Presence of crohn’s disease is a contraindication of surgery in ulcerative colitis due to high rates of fistula formation, obstruction, and leakage."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B, C, D - These are indications for surgery in ulcerative colitis."}]},{"n":9,"q":"Which of the options accurately describes the development of cancer in patients with ulcerative colitis?","o":[{"t":"Bad prognosis if it involved only rectum"},{"t":"Good prognosis if it involves only rectum and is low grade"},{"t":"2% chance of malignancy if present for 10 years"},{"t":"Young age onset has low chances as compared to old age onset"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 2% chance of malignancy if present for 10 years:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2% chance of malignancy if present for 10 years This statement is generally true."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with ulcerative colitis have an increased risk of developing colorectal cancer compared to the general population. The risk is directly related to the duration of the disease. It is estimated that after 10 years of ulcerative colitis, the cumulative risk of developing colorectal cancer is around 2%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, it is important to note that individual risks can vary depending on other factors such as disease severity, extent, and presence of dysplasia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Bad prognosis if it involved only rectum This statement is not entirely accurate. While rectal involvement can increase the risk of cancer development, the prognosis is determined by multiple factors and cannot be solely based on the involvement of the rectum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Good prognosis if it involves only rectum and is low grade- This statement is partially correct. The prognosis of colorectal cancer in ulcerative colitis can vary based on multiple factors, including the extent and grade of the cancer. However, other factors such as the presence of dysplasia, depth of invasion, and lymph node involvement also play a role in determining prognosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Young age onset has low chances as compared to old age onset- This statement is not entirely accurate. Young age onset does not guarantee a low chance of developing cancer, and regular surveillance and monitoring are important regardless of age."}]},{"n":10,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis of a patient who has significant abdominal swelling and laboratory analysis of the fluid obtained through paracentesis reveals SAAG <1.1 what is the likely diagnosis?\n(or)\nWhat is the probable diagnosis of a patient who has significant abdominal swelling and laboratory analysis of the fluid obtained through paracentesis reveals SAAG <1.1 what is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Congestive heart failure"},{"t":"Myxoedema"},{"t":"Budd Chiari syndrome"},{"t":"Tuberculosis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis - SAAG score < 1.1 mg/dl is strongly suggestive of Tuberculosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Congestive heart failure: Congestive heart failure can also lead to the development of ascites. However, in congestive heart failure, the SAAG is usually greater than 1.1 g/dL."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Myxoedema: the SAAG is usually greater than 1.1 g/dL in this condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Budd Chiari syndrome - the SAAG is usually greater than 1.1 g/dL in this condition."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 35-year-old presents with complaints of chest and difficulty in swallowing. Based on the findings in the below image, what is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Achalasia cardia"},{"t":"Diffuse oesophageal spasm"},{"t":"Carcinoma Oesophagus spasm"},{"t":"Schatzki's Ring"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Diffuse oesophageal spasm:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffuse oesophageal spasm is a motility disorder characterized by uncoordinated contractions of the muscles in the esophagus. This condition leads to difficulties in swallowing, chest pain, and sometimes regurgitation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The X-ray image provided shows the characteristic findings associated with Diffuse Oesophageal Spasm, which include multiple contractions along the length of the esophagus, often referred to as \"corkscrew\" or \"rosary bead\" appearance."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Achalasia cardia: The X-ray image does not display the typical findings associated with Achalasia cardia, such as a dilated esophagus or absent peristalsis. Therefore, the image does not support the diagnosis of Achalasia cardia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Carcinoma Oesophagus spasm: The X-ray image does not provide any evidence of a tumor or cancerous growth, which is a characteristic feature of Carcinoma Oesophagus spasm. Therefore, the image does not support the diagnosis of Carcinoma Oesophagus spasm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Schatzki's Ring: The X-ray image does not show any evidence of a narrowing or ring-like structure in the esophagus, which is characteristic of Schatzki's Ring. Thus, the image does not support the diagnosis of Schatzki's Ring."}],"qi":[6547]},{"n":12,"q":"Which of the following actions is not typically performed in cases of Carcinoma of the Oesophagus?","o":[{"t":"Biopsy"},{"t":"pH-metry"},{"t":"CT chest"},{"t":"PET scan"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option B"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH-metry: pH-metry is not typically performed for the evaluation of Carcinoma Oesophagus. pH-metry is a test used to measure the pH levels in the esophagus to evaluate for conditions such as gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) or esophageal motility disorders. It is not specific to Carcinoma Oesophagus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A . Biopsy: A biopsy is typically performed to obtain a tissue sample from the suspected area of carcinoma in the esophagus. The tissue sample is then examined under a microscope to confirm the diagnosis and determine the histological type and grade of the cancer."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. CT chest: A CT (computed tomography) scan of the chest is commonly performed to evaluate the extent and stage of Carcinoma Oesophagus. It helps visualize the size and location of the tumor, as well as any spread to nearby lymph nodes or distant organs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. PET scan: A PET (positron emission tomography) scan is often used to detect the spread of Carcinoma Oesophagus beyond the primary tumor site. It can help identify metastases to distant organs or lymph nodes by detecting areas of increased metabolic activity."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 40-year-old was brought to the emergency with complians of severe abdominal pain with tenderness, fever with chills, and tachycardia. What is the most likely diagnosis for the image displayed?","o":[{"t":"Pneumoperitoneum"},{"t":"Pneumothorax"},{"t":"Eventration of diaphragm"},{"t":"Pneumomediastinum"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Pneumoperitoneum:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct diagnosis based on the given description and the X-ray image showing air under the diaphragm is pneumoperitoneum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumoperitoneum refers to the presence of air or gas within the peritoneal cavity, which is the space between the abdominal organs and the abdominal wall. It is commonly caused by perforation or rupture of a hollow organ in the abdomen, such as the stomach, intestine, or appendix. The presence of air under the diaphragm on an X-ray image is a classic finding seen in pneumoperitoneum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":""},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pneumothorax: Pneumothorax refers to the presence of air or gas in the pleural cavity, which is the space between the lung and the chest wall. It typically appears as air surrounding the lung tissue on an X-ray image."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Eventration of diaphragm: Eventration of the diaphragm is a condition in which the diaphragm muscle is abnormally thin or weak, leading to an elevated and dome-shaped appearance. It does not involve the presence of air under the diaphragm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pneumomediastinum: Pneumomediastinum refers to the presence of air or gas in the mediastinum, which is the central region of the chest between the lungs. It can be caused by various factors, including trauma or medical conditions."}],"qi":[6550]},{"n":14,"q":"Which of the following has the least chance of developing malignancy?\n(or)\nWhich of the following has the least chance of developing malignancy?","o":[{"t":"Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis"},{"t":"Oral submucosal fibrosis"},{"t":"Oral lichen planus"},{"t":"Leukoplakia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Oral lichen planus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral lichen planus (OLP) is a chronic inflammatory condition that affects the mucous membranes in the mouth."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conditions associated with low risk malignant transformation Oral lichen planus Discoid lupus erythematosus Discoid kerratosis congenita"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral lichen planus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Discoid lupus erythematosus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Discoid kerratosis congenita"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral lichen planus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Discoid lupus erythematosus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Discoid kerratosis congenita"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - (Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis) is incorrect because chronic hyperplastic candidiasis, caused by the fungus Candida, is considered a premalignant condition due to its association with an increased risk of oral cancer."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - (Oral submucosal fibrosis) is incorrect because oral submucosal fibrosis, typically caused by the chewing of betel nuts or other irritants, is considered a premalignant condition associated with an increased risk of oral cancer."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - (Leukoplakia) is incorrect because leukoplakia, characterized by white patches in the oral mucosa, is considered a premalignant condition as it has a higher risk of progressing to oral cancer."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 30-year-old female presents with chest pain, recurrent heartburn, and dysphagia. Barium swallow showed a bird beak sign. Which of the following statements is true about the pathology of the condition of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Relaxation of lower esophageal sphincter"},{"t":"Increased peristalsis"},{"t":"Decreased release of NO and VIP in the distal part of esophagus"},{"t":"Decreased tone of the lower esophageal sphincter"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Decreased release of NO and VIP in the distal part of esophagus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Achalasia cardia is a disorder characterized by the dysfunction of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) and impaired esophageal peristalsis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pathology of achalasia cardia involves a decrease in the release of nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) in the distal part of the esophagus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These neurotransmitters are responsible for relaxing the smooth muscles of the esophagus, allowing for proper movement and passage of food."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Relaxation of lower esophageal sphincter:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In achalasia cardia, there is impaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The LES fails to relax properly during swallowing, leading to difficulty in food passage into the stomach."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Increased peristalsis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Achalasia cardia is characterized by ineffective esophageal peristalsis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Decreased tone of the lower esophageal sphincter:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Achalasia cardia is characterized by increased tone of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The LES fails to relax adequately, causing a functional obstruction at the gastroesophageal junction."}]},{"n":16,"q":"What is the accurate statement regarding Crohn's disease?","o":[{"t":"For effective treatment of a Crohn’s mass, only antibiotics are prescribed"},{"t":"Symptomatic relapse after surgery is not related to the presence of disease during resection"},{"t":"The proximal caecum is most commonly involved in Crohn’s disease"},{"t":"On endoscopic examination continuous lesions are visualised, which are diagnostic of Crohn’s disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Symptomatic relapse after surgery is not related to the presence of disease during resection:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crohn's disease is known for its chronic and relapsing nature, and recurrence of symptoms can occur even in areas where surgical resection was performed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This can happen due to the development of new areas of disease involvement or ongoing inflammation in other parts of the gastrointestinal tract."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - For effective treatment of a Crohn’s mass, only antibiotics are prescribed: The treatment of Crohn's disease is comprehensive and typically involves a combination of medications, including anti-inflammatory drugs, immunosuppressants, biologic agents, and sometimes surgical intervention. The specific treatment approach depends on the severity and location of the disease, as well as individual patient factors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - The proximal caecum is most commonly involved in Crohn’s disease: While Crohn's disease can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract from the mouth to the anus, the most common site of involvement is the terminal ileum, which is the distal portion of the small intestine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - On endoscopic examination, continuous lesions are visualized, which are diagnostic of Crohn’s disease: Crohn's disease is characterized by a patchy pattern of inflammation rather than continuous lesions. The inflammation can appear as skip lesions, meaning that there are areas of healthy tissue in between affected areas."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 70-year-old male patient presents with a complaint of dysphagia, particularly for solids, which has been progressively worsening over the past few months. Additionally, he has experienced significant weight loss and cachexia over the same period.Which of the following diagnostic choices is best for this patient?","o":[{"t":"UGIE"},{"t":"Manometry"},{"t":"Barium swallow"},{"t":"Barium meal follow through"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - UGIE:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A patient with dysphagia more for solids than liquids and cachexia likely indicates carcinoma esophagus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Upper GI endoscopy is performed on the patient to visualize and identify the tumor and also to obtain a tissue biopsy to confirm the diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A thorough endoscopic inspection of the larynx, trachea, and bronchi is conducted as the population at risk has a high chance of carcinoma of the lung, head, and neck region."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT scan of the chest and abdomen is used to detect the extent of the tumor spread to the mediastinum and para-aortic lymph nodes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Manometry:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Manometry is used in the diagnosis of esophageal motility disorders."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Barium swallow:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Barium swallow may be used to study the mucosal lining of the esophagus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Barium meal follow-through:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Barium meal follow-through is used in diagnosis of the lower GIT abnormalities"}]},{"n":18,"q":"What is the underlying reason for the arterial blood gas (ABG) findings in a diabetic woman who has been admitted to the hospital due to continuous vomiting after consuming food from outside, and has a blood pressure reading of 90/60 mmHg? pH-7.52 HCO3 – 30 meq/L PaCO2 - 20 mmHg Na – 123 mEq/L K – 3.2 mEq/L CI – 67 mEq/L","o":[{"t":"Diabetic ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Persistent vomiting"},{"t":"Septic shock"},{"t":"Renal tubular acidosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Persistent vomiting:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Persistent vomiting can lead to a condition called metabolic alkalosis. When a person vomits, they lose stomach acid (hydrochloric acid), which results in a decrease in hydrogen ions (H+). This loss of acid leads to an imbalance in the body's acid-base status, causing the blood pH to increase (become more alkaline)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA): DKA is a serious complication of diabetes characterized by high blood sugar levels, ketone production, and metabolic acidosis.Therefore, DKA is not the cause of the ABG findings."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Septic shock: Septic shock can lead to multiple organ dysfunction and a state of metabolic acidosis. The pH of 7.52 in the ABG report suggests alkalosis rather than acidosis, so septic shock is unlikely to be the cause."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Renal tubular acidosis (RTA): RTA is a condition characterized by a defect in the kidneys' ability to excrete acid, leading to a buildup of acid in the body. Since the ABG report shows alkalosis with a pH of 7.52, RTA is not the likely cause."}]},{"n":19,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a patient being assessed for jaundice, based on laboratory findings of increased bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase levels, while the levels of other liver enzymes remain within normal range?","o":[{"t":"Obstructive jaundice"},{"t":"Hemolytic jaundice"},{"t":"Hepatic jaundice"},{"t":"Prehepatic jaundice"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Obstructive jaundice:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase levels are suggestive of obstructive jaundice , which happens when there's hindrance of the biliary tree."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obstruction can happen due to different reasons, such as gallstones, tumors, or strictures. Alkaline phosphatase levels raise due to the increased generation of the enzyme by the liver and biliary system, whereas bilirubin elevated due to the collection of conjugated bilirubin within the bloodstream, which cannot be excreted through the bile duct."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hemolytic jaundice: Hemolytic jaundice is caused by the excessive breakdown of red blood cells, driving an increment in unconjugated bilirubin levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hepatic jaundice: Hepatic jaundice happens due to liver cell damage, driving to impeded bilirubin processing and excretion ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Prehepatic jaundice: Prehepatic jaundice happens due to excessive red blood cell breakdown, driving an increment in unconjugated bilirubin levels."}]},{"n":20,"q":"Patient is identified as having a tumor in the ileum leading to urinary 5 HIAA being elevated. What is the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Carcinoid tumor"},{"t":"Neuroblastoma"},{"t":"Leiomyoma"},{"t":"Chromaffinoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A - Carcinoid tumors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Carcinoid tumors are a type of neuroendocrine tumor that can arise in various parts of the body, including the gastrointestinal tract (especially the ileum), lungs, and pancreas. They are usually slow-growing but can metastasize to other organs over time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"One of the hallmark features of carcinoid tumors is the secretion of serotonin, which can lead to elevated levels of urinary 5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (5-HIAA). This is because serotonin is converted to 5-HIAA in the liver and then excreted in the urine."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Neuroblastoma is most commonly diagnosed in infants and young children and can cause symptoms such as abdominal swelling or pain, fever, and weight loss. Option C: Leiomyoma is a benign tumor arising from smooth muscle tissue and can occur in various parts of the body (including the uterus, gastrointestinal tract, and skin)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Chromaffinoma (also known as a pheochromocytoma) is a type of tumor arising from chromaffin cells in the adrenal glands and sympathetic ganglia (part of the autonomic nervous system)."}]},{"n":21,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a 50-year-old woman with a skin lesion visible in the provided image, which initially appeared small but has now spread to her entire limb and abdomen? Despite being on anticoagulation therapy, she has a longstanding history of diabetes and recurring deep vein thrombosis episodes.","o":[{"t":"Insulinoma"},{"t":"Gastrinoma"},{"t":"Glucagonoma"},{"t":"VIPoma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Glucagonoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis for this patient's condition is glucagonoma. A glucagonoma is a rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor that produces excessive amounts of glucagon. Clinical features of glucagonoma Diabetes mellitus/impaired glucose tolerance Diarrhea Dermatological features Migratory necrolytic erythema (red rash- bullae- erosions) Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus/impaired glucose tolerance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diarrhea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dermatological features Migratory necrolytic erythema (red rash- bullae- erosions)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Migratory necrolytic erythema (red rash- bullae- erosions)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A - An insulinoma is a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor that secretes excessive amounts of insulin. This condition can lead to hypoglycemia, which can cause symptoms such as weakness, confusion, sweating, and palpitations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option B - A gastrinoma is a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor that produces excessive amounts of gastrin, a hormone that stimulates the production of stomach acid. This can cause symptoms such as abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option D - While VIPomas can cause skin lesions, they are not typically associated with recurrent deep vein thrombosis or long-standing diabetes, so it is an unlikely diagnosis in this case."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus/impaired glucose tolerance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diarrhea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dermatological features Migratory necrolytic erythema (red rash- bullae- erosions)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Migratory necrolytic erythema (red rash- bullae- erosions)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Migratory necrolytic erythema (red rash- bullae- erosions)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"}],"qi":[6555]},{"n":22,"q":"Mark the correct statement regarding inflammatory bowel disease.","o":[{"t":"Skip lesions are present in Crohn’s disease"},{"t":"Mucosal layers are involved in Crohn’s while transmural involvement seen in ulcerative colitis"},{"t":"Inflammatory bowel disease doesn’t have a genetic predisposition"},{"t":"Crohn’s is curable through surgical resection of the affected segment"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Skip lesions are present in Crohn’s disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skip lesions refer to the characteristic feature of Crohn's disease where there are areas of inflammation that are separated by normal or unaffected segments of the bowel. This pattern of involvement is not seen in ulcerative colitis. Skip lesions can occur throughout the entire gastrointestinal tract, from the mouth to the anus, in Crohn's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Mucosal layers are involved in Crohn's while transmural involvement seen in ulcerative colitis: This statement is incorrect. In Crohn's disease, the inflammation can involve all layers of the bowel wall, which is known as transmural involvement. In ulcerative colitis, the inflammation is limited to the mucosal layer of the colon and rectum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Inflammatory bowel disease does have a strong genetic predisposition: This statement is incorrect. Both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis have a strong genetic component, indicating a predisposition to developing these conditions. Family history of inflammatory bowel disease is a known risk factor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Crohn's is curable through surgical resection of the affected segment: This statement is incorrect. Crohn's disease is a chronic condition that requires long-term management and treatment to control inflammation and maintain remission."}]},{"n":23,"q":"A patient presents with cough, cold, fever and malaise associated with weight loss and ascites. A radiograph of the chest is shown below. Ascitic fluid analysis showed elevated adenosine deaminase. Which of the following is the next step in management?","o":[{"t":"Biopsy"},{"t":"Start ATT after laparotomy stricture removal"},{"t":"Start anti-tubercular therapy"},{"t":"Symptomatic management"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Start anti-tubercular therapy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest x-ray and clinical symptoms are suggestive of tuberculous peritonitis secondary to pulmonary tuberculosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High levels of adenosine deaminase, lysozyme, and IFN-gamma may suggest a tuberculous etiology."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is typically managed with a combination of anti-tubercular drugs, including isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Biopsy: Biopsy may be considered if the diagnosis is uncertain or to establish the definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Start ATT after laparotomy stricture removal: Laparotomy stricture removal is not indicated in the management of tuberculous peritonitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Symptomatic management: Symptomatic management is inadequate and initiation of anti-tubercular therapy is a more appropriate option"}],"qi":[6558]},{"n":24,"q":"A patient with peptic ulcer was treated with an H. pylori regimen. Which of the following is used to assess the success of treatment?","o":[{"t":"Endoscopy"},{"t":"IgG antibody study"},{"t":"Urea breath test"},{"t":"Urease test of biopsy"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Urea breath test:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is the preferred method to assess the success of H. pylori treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The urea breath test, the stool antigen test, and biopsy-based tests can all be used to assess the success of the treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, their use <4 weeks after treatment may yield false-negative results."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B and D are incorrect. Urea breath test is used to determine treatment response in patients on anti H.pylori regimens."}]},{"n":25,"q":"What is the enzyme deficiency in Pompe’s disease?","o":[{"t":"Lysosomal acid alpha glucosidase"},{"t":"Glucose 6 phosphatase"},{"t":"Muscle phosphorylase"},{"t":"Phosphofructokinase"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lysosomal acid alpha glucosidase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pompe's disease, also known as glycogen storage disease type II, is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme lysosomal acid alpha glucosidase, also called acid alpha-glucosidase or acid maltase."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen, a stored form of glucose, into glucose molecules within the lysosomes of cells."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Glucose 6 phosphatase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucose 6 phosphatase is an enzyme involved in gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis. Deficiency of this enzyme is seen in Von Gierke's disease (glycogen storage disease type I), not in Pompe's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Muscle phosphorylase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle phosphorylase, also known as myophosphorylase, is an enzyme involved in glycogenolysis (the breakdown of glycogen into glucose) in muscle tissue. Deficiency of muscle phosphorylase leads to McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V), characterized by exercise intolerance and muscle cramps."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Phosphofructokinase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phosphofructokinase is an enzyme involved in glycolysis, the breakdown of glucose to produce energy. Deficiency of phosphofructokinase leads to Tarui disease (glycogen storage disease type VII), characterized by exercise intolerance and muscle weakness."}]},{"n":26,"q":"A 10-year-old child presented with recurrent pulmonary infections, and bulky greasy stools. Quantitative estimation of the stool fat was more than 10 gm/day. Which of the following will be seen in this child?","o":[{"t":"Protein-losing enteropathy"},{"t":"Distal intestinal obstruction"},{"t":"Hypernatremia"},{"t":"Rectal prolapse occurs after treatment"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Distal intestinal obstruction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The obstruction hinders the proper absorption of fats and nutrients, resulting in the passage of undigested fats in the stool."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs and symptoms of DIOS include a sudden onset of crampy abdominal pain, vomiting, and a palpable mass (often in the right lower quadrant) in the abdomen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-rays of the abdomen may reveal stool in the colon and air-fluid levels in the small intestines."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Protein-losing enteropathy: Protein-losing enteropathy can result in edema, hypoalbuminemia, and other signs of protein deficiency. While it can cause changes in stool consistency, such as frothy or pale stools, it is not typically associated with bulky greasy stools."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypernatremia: Hypernatremia refers to elevated levels of sodium in the blood. It is not directly associated with the symptoms described in the case, which are more suggestive of gastrointestinal malabsorption."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Rectal prolapse occurs after treatment: Rectal prolapse is a condition where the rectum protrudes through the anus. While it can occur in some cases of chronic diarrhea and malabsorption, it is not directly related to the symptoms mentioned in the case."}]},{"n":27,"q":"What is the most probable diagnosis for a patient with a past medical record of chronic liver disease, who is currently experiencing abdominal distension, jaundice, and itching, and whose ascitic fluid analysis shows a neutrophil count exceeding 650 per cubic mm?","o":[{"t":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"},{"t":"Malignant ascites"},{"t":"Tubercular ascites"},{"t":"Intestinal obstruction"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case scenario is diagnostic of Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis may present with abdominal pain, rebound tenderness, absent bowel sounds and fever in a patient with obvious features of cirrhosis and ascites."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic paracentesis may show cloudy fluid, and an ascites neutrophil count >250 x 109/L almost invariably indicates infection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Malignant ascites: It is a complication of cancer, and the presence of neutrophils in the ascitic fluid would be different."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tubercular ascites: It is an infrequent proof of infection and usually guides a lymphocytic predominance in the ascitic fluid."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Intestinal obstruction: It is an independent condition that presents accompanying manifestations including abdominal pain, vomiting, and constipation ."}]},{"n":28,"q":"A 15-year-old girl presented with fatigue, chronic diarrhoea, weight loss, bone pain and abdominal distension. Investigations revealed iron deficiency anaemia and osteoporosis. Which of the following is the single best test to be done for her evaluation?","o":[{"t":"TSH levels"},{"t":"C-peptide levels"},{"t":"Urine sugar and ketone"},{"t":"IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct answer is IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody (IgA-TTG) testing. This test is used to evaluate for celiac disease, an autoimmune condition characterized by an immune reaction to gluten, a protein found in wheat, barley, and rye."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Celiac disease can lead to malabsorption, chronic diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and other symptoms mentioned in the case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - TSH levels: Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels are typically measured to evaluate thyroid function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - C-peptide levels: C-peptide is a marker of insulin production and can be measured to assess pancreatic function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Urine sugar and ketone: Testing urine for sugar and ketones is often performed to evaluate for diabetes mellitus or diabetic ketoacidosis."}]},{"n":29,"q":"The blood investigation of a patient is given below. What is the probable diagnosis? HBsAg- Nonreactive HBeAg- Nonreactive IgG anti-HbcAg – Reactive IgM anti-HbcAg – Nonreactive","o":[{"t":"Acute hepatitis B"},{"t":"Chronic hepatitis B"},{"t":"Remote infection of hepatitis B"},{"t":"Core window period"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Remote infection of hepatitis B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IgG anti-HBcAg (IgG antibody to hepatitis B core antigen) reactive: The presence of IgG anti-HBcAg indicates a previous or remote infection with HBV. IgG antibodies to HBcAg persist after the acute phase of infection and can provide long-term immunity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - The patient does not have acute hepatitis B because the key markers for active infection (HBsAg and HBeAg) are non-reactive. Therefore, acute hepatitis B cannot be the diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Based on these findings, the patient does not have chronic hepatitis B because the key markers for active infection (HBsAg and HBeAg) are non-reactive. Therefore chronic hepatitis B cannot be the diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - The core window period is not applicable in this case, as the core window period refers to a specific phase of hepatitis B infection. During the window period, there may be a temporary absence or low levels of detectable hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) and hepatitis B e antigen (HBeAg) in the blood, while the person may still have detectable IgM antibodies to hepatitis B core antigen (IgM anti-HBcAg)."}]},{"n":30,"q":"A male patient in his youth comes to the clinic complaining of a persistent high fever over the past 5 days. During the examination of his hands, painless lesions are observed, and a new regurgitant murmur is detected. The echocardiogram reveals the existence of a mass on the mitral valve. Based on these findings, which of the following abdominal findings would be expected in this case?","o":[{"t":"Ascites"},{"t":"Splenomegaly"},{"t":"Hepatomegaly"},{"t":"Portal hypertension"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Splenomegaly:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical presentation, the most likely abdomen finding in this case would be splenomegaly. The correct answer is: Splenomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of high-grade fever, painless lesions on the hands (janeway lesions), and a new regurgitant murmur suggests the possibility of infective endocarditis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly is a common finding in infective endocarditis due to septic emboli. As the infected emboli reach the spleen, they can cause local inflammation and enlargement of the organ."}],"qi":[6566]},{"n":31,"q":"What clinical examination is being performed in the image?","o":[{"t":"Shifting dullness"},{"t":"Ascites"},{"t":"Puddle sign"},{"t":"Fluid thrill"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Fluid thrill:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid Thrill: Fluid thrill, also known as fluid wave, is another technique used to detect ascites. The patient or an assistant places their hand firmly on the midline of the abdomen, and the examiner taps one side of the abdomen sharply with their hand or fingers. If asciticfluid is present, a palpable wave of fluid can be felt across the abdomen."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Shifting Dullness: Shifting dullness is a maneuver used to assess the presence of fluid in the abdomen."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ascites: Ascites refers to the accumulation of excess fluid in the peritoneal cavity, resulting in abdominal distension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Puddle Sign: The puddle sign is an extension of the shifting dullness maneuver."}],"qi":[6572]},{"n":32,"q":"Irits is associated with all of the below conditions except.","o":[{"t":"SLE"},{"t":"Behcet’s disease"},{"t":"Ulcerative colitis"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Rheumatoid arthritis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iritis, also known as anterior uveitis, is inflammation of the iris, which is the colored part of the eye. It can occur as a result of various underlying conditions. Among the options provided, rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is not typically associated with iritis. But conjunctiva and sclera are involved in RA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Sarcoidosis- : Sarcoidosis is a multisystemic inflammatory disorder involving >2 organ. Iritis is occular manifestation in sarcoidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Behcet's disease: Behcet's disease is a rare autoimmune condition characterized by recurrent oral and genital ulcers, skin lesions, and uveitis. Uveitis, including iritis, is a well-known ocular manifestation of Behcet's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Ulcerative colitis: Ulcerative colitis is a type of inflammatory bowel disease that primarily affects the colon and rectum. While its primary manifestations are related to the gastrointestinal system, extraintestinal manifestations can occur, including ocular involvement. Uveitis, including iritis, can occur in patients with ulcerative colitis."}]},{"n":33,"q":"Except for which of the following, all the mentioned characteristics are associated with Crohn's disease?","o":[{"t":"Transmural involvement"},{"t":"Lead pipe appearance"},{"t":"Rectal sparing"},{"t":"Perianal fistula"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Lead pipe appearance:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lead pipe appearance: This feature is not typically associated with Crohn's disease. The term \" lead pipe appearance\" is used to describe the smooth, uniform narrowing of the colon seen in conditions like ulcerative colitis. In Crohn's disease, the involvement can be patchy and may lead to skip lesions or segmental involvement rather than a continuous, uniform appearance."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Transmural involvement : This is a characteristic feature of Crohn's disease. It refers to inflammation that extends through all layers of the intestinal wall, including the mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and serosa."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Rectal sparing : This is a feature seen in Crohn's disease. It refers to the relative sparing of the rectum from inflammation. Crohn's disease often involves the terminal ileum (the last part of the small intestine) and can extend to other parts of the gastrointestinal tract, but the rectum is commonly spared."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Perianal fistula: Perianal fistulas are frequently associated with Crohn's disease. These abnormal connections between the intestine and the skin around the anus can cause pain, discharge, and recurrent infections."}]},{"n":34,"q":"A 40-year-old patient was brought to the OPD with complains of fatigability, vomiting, and malaise. Examination revealed occult blood in stools, anemia, and hypertension. The patient was diagnosed to have cushing’s ulcers. What could be the likely reason for cushion’s ulcer?","o":[{"t":"Burns"},{"t":"Stress"},{"t":"Head injury"},{"t":"Cell necrosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Head injury :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cushing's ulcers can develop in patients with severe head injuries, particularly those with elevated intracranial pressure. The increased intracranial pressure leads to activation of the vagus nerve , which stimulates the release of gastric acid and increases gastric motility, resulting in the development of gastric ulcers."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A. Burns : Burns are not typically associated with the development of Cushing's ulcers. Gastric ulcers in burn patients are more commonly attributed to other factors such as stress, decreased mucosal blood flow, and altered acid secretion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B. Stress : While stress can contribute to the development of gastric ulcers, it is not specific to Cushing's ulcers. Stress-related ulcers, also known as stress ulcers, can occur in various conditions such as severe illness, trauma, surgery, or critical illness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. Cell necrosis : Cell necrosis alone is not a direct cause of Cushing's ulcers. The development of gastric ulcers involves complex mechanisms related to the autonomic nervous system and altered acid secretion."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"6566":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6572":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6547":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6550":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6555":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6558":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});